<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
  <!--
    Two support arms that open OUTWARD along the tool's X axis, which is how the
    real 夹持机构0228 braces the module's handles.

    Geometry is measured, not invented.  In the flange frame the real arms are:

      left   arm  X [-147.31, -27.31]  Y [-9.03, 48.97]  Z [  4.0, 150.0] mm
      right  arm  X [ 113.29, 147.29]  Y [-9.03, 48.97]  Z [  4.0, 150.0] mm

    each 58 mm deep and 146 mm tall.  What matters for the grasp is the OUTER
    face of each arm (the surface that presses on a handle's inner face): the
    module's handle inner span is 244.00 mm, so with zero interference the arms
    open to +/-122.00 mm.  Each arm therefore starts retracted at +/-102.00 mm
    and travels outward to the contact plane.

    These are NOT a model of the real linkage.  They are the real contact plane,
    travel direction and cross-section carried by a simplified slab, so the
    opening motion and the contact geometry are honest while the link and joint
    names that four other files depend on survive:

      - cs625_active_perception.srdf names these links in disable_collisions,
      - sim_controllers.yaml drives gripper_right_finger_joint,
      - test/p7_apply_fixture_scene.py uses FINGER_LINKS by name.

    TRAVEL (aligned 2026-10-24).  Only two aperture numbers exist for this tool,
    and both are CAD measurements made by the tool owner, not bench
    measurements of the assembled gripper:

      j = 0            outer-face span 204.000 mm   retracted.  NOT MEASURED:
                                                    the retracted span is a
                                                    derived value (contact span
                                                    minus the 20 mm travel), so
                                                    this end of the range is
                                                    UNVERIFIED.
      j = 0.020000     outer-face span 244.000 mm   = the module's measured
                                                    handle inner span.  This is
                                                    the zero-preload grasp pose
                                                    chosen by the task owner
                                                    (see config/cs625_task_scene.yaml,
                                                    interference_fit: none_zero_preload).
      j = 0.021285     pad displacement (NOT the gripper opening)
                                                    between the tool's two
                                                    parallel arm faces
                                                    (cs625_task_scene.yaml,
                                                    measured_arm_span_m: 0.24657).

    Aperture mapping used below, exact for this slab model:

      span_outer_mm(j) = 204.0 + 2000.0 * j        (j in metres)
      j(span_outer_mm) = (span_outer_mm - 204.0) / 2000.0

    Read the number carefully: the real gripper opens 50 mm in total (owner,
    verified on the hardware).  This joint does NOT model that opening - it models
    how far each support pad moves outwards when the brace linkage converts the
    50 mm stroke, i.e. (246.570 - 204.000) / 2 = 21.285 mm per pad.  The brace
    ratio is therefore 42.57/50 = 0.8514 (span per mm of opening).

    The upper limit was 0.020, which is the zero-preload contact, i.e. the model
    stopped 2.57 mm short of the measured full aperture.  It is now 0.021285 so
    the joint range reaches the measured span; the grasp itself still commands
    0.020, and the extra 1.285 mm per side is exactly the real preload that a
    rigid-body contact deliberately does not apply.

    STILL NOT ALIGNED, because no number exists to align with: the HITBOT EFG80
    drive coordinate that config/tasks/common.yaml calls grip_width_mm: 50.0.
    The drive's position_mm (0..80 command range) is a serial/encoder coordinate,
    and docs/agent_memory.md section 76 records that this displayed position does
    NOT equal the physical opening ("归零后有偏移误差，显示位置与实际开度不完全
    一致").  No log maps drive millimetres to a jaw span, so 50 mm CANNOT be
    converted into a span here.  Any statement that "the simulator opens to
    50 mm" would be an invention.  MISSING DATA: measured outer-face span at a
    set of drive positions (calibration table), plus the retracted span.

    MASS: ESTIMATED, needs CAD mass/CoG.  These are NOT measured values.
    my_end_effector_link already carries the MEASURED 4.580 kg of the same rigid
    body (SolidWorks export 夹持机构0920, see cs625_active_perception.urdf.xacro)
    and these links sit inside that body, so every kilogram put here is counted
    twice.  The values below are deliberately small bookkeeping estimates
    (0.100 + 2 x 0.020 = 0.140 kg, a 3.1% duplicate) whose only job is to give
    the tree physically sane inertials instead of 1e-9 placeholders.  The
    physically plausible split still has to come from CAD: a SOLID arm slab of
    0.055 x 0.058 x 0.146 m at the measured 2.677 g/cm^3 aluminium density would
    be 1.246 kg (certainly hollow in reality), plus an unknown housing share of
    the remaining 2.09 kg.  Do not quote the numbers below as the gripper mass;
    the gripper mass is 4.580 kg and it lives on my_end_effector_link.

    effort = 80 N and velocity = 0.02 m/s are also UNVERIFIED: the real gripper
    is run current-limited at 0.1 A (config/tasks/common.yaml grip_current), and
    no force or speed measurement exists in this repository.

    ZERO INTERFERENCE was chosen deliberately.  The real tool closes with 2.57 mm
    of preload, which a rigid-body contact cannot reproduce honestly: it would
    either explode or, with the arms placed exactly at contact, leave the normal
    force to the solver.  The second is what is modelled, and it is recorded as a
    known limitation in config/cs625_task_scene.yaml rather than hidden.
  -->
  <xacro:macro name="cs625_parallel_gripper" params="parent prefix">
    <!-- Frame at the tool mounting face.  Kept so the joint name survives. -->
    <link name="${prefix}gripper_base_link">
      <inertial>
        <!-- ESTIMATED, needs CAD mass/CoG.  Not measured.  Bookkeeping share of
             the 4.580 kg body that already sits on my_end_effector_link; a
             90 x 90 x 50 mm envelope with the CoM left at the mounting face.
             Inertia is the solid-box value for 0.100 kg at that envelope. -->
        <mass value="0.100"/>
        <inertia ixx="8.83e-05" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="8.83e-05" iyz="0" izz="1.35e-04"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${prefix}tool0_to_gripper_base" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${parent}"/>
      <child link="${prefix}gripper_base_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Right arm: outer face at joint x = 0, so the slab extends in -X. -->
    <link name="${prefix}gripper_right_finger_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="-0.0275 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.055 0.058 0.146"/></geometry>
        <material name="gripper_arm"><color rgba="0.55 0.55 0.58 1"/></material>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="-0.0275 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.055 0.058 0.146"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <!-- ESTIMATED, needs CAD mass/CoG.  Not measured.  CoM at the slab
             centre; inertia is the solid-box value for 0.020 kg over
             0.055 x 0.058 x 0.146 m.  See the MASS note above: 0.020 kg is a
             bookkeeping duplicate, not the arm's real mass (a solid slab of
             this size would be 1.246 kg). -->
        <origin xyz="-0.0275 0 0"/>
        <mass value="0.020"/>
        <inertia ixx="4.11e-05" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="4.06e-05" iyz="0" izz="1.07e-05"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${prefix}gripper_right_finger_joint" type="prismatic">
      <parent link="${prefix}gripper_base_link"/>
      <child link="${prefix}gripper_right_finger_link"/>
      <!-- Retracted at 102.0 mm; 21.285 mm of travel reaches the measured
           123.285 mm = 246.57 mm aperture.  0.020 is the zero-preload grasp. -->
      <origin xyz="0.102 0 0.077" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
      <limit lower="0.0" upper="0.021285" effort="80" velocity="0.02"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Left arm: mirrored; outer face at joint x = 0, slab extends in +X. -->
    <link name="${prefix}gripper_left_finger_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.0275 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.055 0.058 0.146"/></geometry>
        <material name="gripper_arm_left"><color rgba="0.55 0.55 0.58 1"/></material>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0.0275 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.055 0.058 0.146"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <!-- ESTIMATED, needs CAD mass/CoG.  Mirror of the right arm. -->
        <origin xyz="0.0275 0 0"/>
        <mass value="0.020"/>
        <inertia ixx="4.11e-05" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="4.06e-05" iyz="0" izz="1.07e-05"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${prefix}gripper_left_finger_joint" type="prismatic">
      <parent link="${prefix}gripper_base_link"/>
      <child link="${prefix}gripper_left_finger_link"/>
      <origin xyz="-0.102 0 0.077" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="-1 0 0"/>
      <mimic joint="${prefix}gripper_right_finger_joint" multiplier="1.0" offset="0.0"/>
      <limit lower="0.0" upper="0.021285" effort="80" velocity="0.02"/>
    </joint>

    <ros2_control name="${prefix}cs625_parallel_gripper" type="system">
      <hardware><plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin></hardware>
      <joint name="${prefix}gripper_right_finger_joint">
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="position"><param name="initial_value">0.0</param></state_interface>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/><state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>
      <joint name="${prefix}gripper_left_finger_joint">
        <state_interface name="position"/><state_interface name="velocity"/><state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>
    </ros2_control>
  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
